DarthBob88 wrote:
> Personally, I prefer an Emerson quotation:"A foolish consistency is the
> hobgoblin of little minds[...]", which Isaac Asimov also liked. As for
> my logic, applying grease to the outside of an invisibility cloak,
> which makes things under it invisible, should produce the illusion of
> grease floating through the air, and neatly cancelling his
> invisibility. This may be wrong, but it seems rational to me.

Exactly.  To think otherwise would be to expect anything you bumped
into or touched while wearing an invisibility cloak to also become
invisible.

But you're absolutely right: how often is one able to apply "common
sense" to a situation in NetHack?  Rarely.  Thus, I asked the question.
 Discussing the repercussions of greasing a cloak of invisibility seems
to me anything but unimaginative.  NetHack itself is one big ASCII
fantasy, which, I expect, would not appeal to anyone unwilling to use
his or her imagination.



